WEBVTT

00:00:00.526 --> 00:00:26.894
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. It's Monday, so we're in our Visual Arts and Heritage stretch. Today on the show: Van Gogh's Châtaigniers en fleurs sold for a record $190 million after a Nazi-era claim was settled days before the hammer fell, and we ask whether a two-sentence settlement is enough of a record. Plus Quesnel's pulp mill halting indefinitely, and a Cariboo Chilcotin museum worth knowing about. Stay with us.

00:00:40.369 --> 00:01:00.849
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Monday, November 23rd, 2026. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:01.849 --> 00:01:29.169
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're asking who keeps the record of the things people make, and who gets to say where those things came from. Weather first. It's around minus 4 in Williams Lake this morning, partly cloudy, with a high of plus 1 and a southeast wind gusting to 50 kilometres an hour, so the wind chill is minus 11 this morning. In 100 Mile House expect a chance of flurries and a high of minus 1, and Quesnel gets a mix of sun and cloud with a high of 1.

00:01:29.541 --> 00:01:31.309
<v Riley>That's the weather. On to the news.

00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:38.589
<v Riley>A lot of today's news lands in our own backyard, and most of it is about who makes things and who keeps them.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:02:13.460
<v Riley>Quesnel's pulp mill is in an indefinite halt. Millar Western is curtailing production at the Quesnel River Pulp mill, according to a Canadian Press report carried by Pique Newsmagazine. Workers have been off since September, when the company first shut the mill temporarily. Premier-designate Lorne Doerkson called the news a "hard blow" for workers and their families, and said the company has "left the door open" to a restart. He also said mills need faster access to fibre and permits to keep running, and warned there is no overnight fix.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.838
<v Casey>Off since September, with no restart date. Every shop on Quesnel's main street feels that.

00:02:20.638 --> 00:02:50.732
<v Casey>The Quesnel Cariboo Observer ran two pieces on shopping local this week. One profiles shop owners, and the other is a holiday gift guide. Lisa Foottit has owned Mama C's Gifts &amp; Giggles for just over five years, and Denise Rosetto runs The Crafty Nook. Both stock work from local makers, including jewellery and honey from a local producer. Cariboo Keepsakes Local Arts and Crafts is another shop the paper names, with handmade pieces from the region. The pitch is simple: a gift from a local maker keeps the craft and the money in town.

00:02:52.218 --> 00:03:19.954
<v Riley>100 Mile House's free counselling program has moved to the Canadian Mental Health Association South Cariboo Branch. The program ran out of the Cariboo Family Enrichment Centre, which closed earlier this month. Counsellors Tyler Dixon and Elaine Wanner-Van Osch both worked at the centre for several years, and they are now taking new clients. Dixon has a background in family support and social work. Wanner-Van Osch holds a master's in psychology.

00:03:21.511 --> 00:03:50.927
<v Casey>Students at 100 Mile Elementary took part in the CIVIX Student Vote. The student vote produced a minority Conservative government, with Doerkson as premier. Across B.C., more than 850 schools took part, with about 100,000 students casting ballots on the same Saturday, October 24th, as the adults. The 100 Mile class ran the election itself, with the Grade 5 and 6 students handling the voting jobs. The student results differed from the real vote in several key ways.

00:03:52.516 --> 00:04:31.290
<v Riley>Inside Gaza won Best Documentary and an audience award at the Cinemania festival in Montreal on November 14th. The 72-minute film follows four journalists from Agence France-Presse, the French news agency, during the first seven months after the October 7th, 2023 attack on Israel. Directed by Hélène Lam Trong, it is built mostly from footage the journalists shot themselves, about 85 percent of the film. AFP's Gaza bureau chief, Adel Zaanoun, accepted the awards and dedicated them to journalists killed in the war and to those still working there. The film is also eligible for the 2027 Oscars.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:36.676
<v Casey>Most of it is their own footage, because the territory is largely closed to international media.

00:04:38.375 --> 00:05:11.277
<v Casey>In Egypt, a court ordered the independent outlet Mada Masr closed in late October. The court also fined its editor-in-chief, Lina Attalah, 3 million Egyptian pounds, about US$54,000, and ordered its equipment confiscated, for operating an unlicensed website. The outlet says it has applied for a licence repeatedly without success. The Committee to Protect Journalists reports that Egypt's Supreme Judicial Council rejected claims of procedural violations, and that Attalah waived her right to appeal, which her defense denies. The first appeal session is scheduled for November 25th.

00:05:11.726 --> 00:05:14.670
<v Riley>They can't get a licence, and running without one is the offence.

00:05:16.405 --> 00:05:39.725
<v Riley>Federal funding for public media may come back in some form, according to Nieman Lab. The U.S. Corporation for Public Broadcasting, the main funder of public media in the United States since 1967, has ceased to exist after Republican budget cuts. Nieman Lab's question is whether Congress revives the old system or builds something better suited to today's media ecosystem.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:53.772
<v Casey>A lab-grown product called Leaven is winning over high-end bakers and pizza makers, the people who built their reputations on live cultures. The New York Times framed it as whether an "A.I. agent" is coming for the sourdough starter.

00:05:55.361 --> 00:06:09.273
<v Riley>Maria Tomazou made a short animated film in Microsoft Excel. FlowingData posted it as a reminder that a data tool doesn't have to produce work that looks like the tool. Tomazou's own line on it: "I don't need to know the why for everything."

00:06:10.688 --> 00:06:37.312
<v Casey>Being Heumann premiered on Apple TV on November 13th. Director Siân Heder's film tells the story of Judy Heumann, and of the activists who pushed the government in the spring of 1977 to enforce Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973. NPR spoke with a new generation of disability rights activists about carrying that work forward. The film argues Heumann is less known than many other civil rights activists of her time.

00:06:38.743 --> 00:06:56.407
<v Riley>The Marginalian resurfaced an older piece on the cellist Pablo Casals, quoting him at age 93. "The man who works and is never bored is never old," he said. Work and interest in worthwhile things, he added, are the best remedy for age. The piece comes from the reader-supported newsletter's archive.

00:06:57.921 --> 00:07:17.529
<v Casey>Engadget says the best solar panel depends on more than efficiency. Roof space, shade, budget and what the utility pays for surplus power all change the math. Its suggested rule is to size a system to produce about 20 percent more than the household normally uses, as a buffer for dimmer days. Inverters, mounting hardware and labour count too.

00:07:18.959 --> 00:07:44.335
<v Riley>Nik Ivanov built Mural 2.0, a large-format pen plotter that draws murals on a wall. The machine hangs from a single belt anchored at ceiling level, and the design loses about 20 percent of the area at the edges. The real limit is the marker. Past about 500 metres of line, the pen dries out and the strokes get weak. Ivanov is working on a pause-and-replace routine for the next firmware.

00:07:44.784 --> 00:07:47.328
<v Casey>The wall is the cheap part. The marker is the bill.

00:07:49.052 --> 00:08:07.964
<v Casey>Mona Chalabi, a Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist and artist, talks with Hyperallergic about her first book, Ten Lives. It's six years of research, interviews and illustration on class, in her visual style. She says her work isn't fine art, and the interview argues it's close to the purest version of what art should do: change how we see.

00:08:09.475 --> 00:08:38.667
<v Riley>Eleanor Antin, 91, is suing her former dealer, Richard Saltoun, in New York State Supreme Court. Her complaint, filed in July, seeks at least 179 works back, or $975,000 in compensatory damages. It says Saltoun agreed at a London meeting in January to return everything. He had said in February that all the works went back to the family. An inventory that he produced later showed the works still missing, most of them held at the London gallery.

00:08:39.117 --> 00:08:44.093
<v Casey>The count came from an inventory Saltoun produced himself. That's the detail that matters.

00:08:45.834 --> 00:09:07.058
<v Casey>Scientific American reports on research with animals that may show where music began. California sea lions and cockatoos are among the animals giving scientists clues. One sea lion, Ronan, at the University of California, Santa Cruz, responds to hand signals with a tongue pop, works food puzzles, and has been known to hoard Jell-O rewards so she can keep taking part in research.

00:09:08.487 --> 00:09:27.559
<v Riley>The Atlantic ran an essay arguing that attempts to rebuild a lost past may be doomed to fail, but are still noble, and sometimes beautiful. Its example is MacDiarmid, who didn't rebuild Scottish literature but made a modernist work of art. Rebuilding a past depends on what survived. That's where we're going next.

00:09:28.959 --> 00:09:32.911
<v Riley>Next up is a local institution that holds part of the region's own record.

00:09:33.555 --> 00:09:50.307
<v Casey>The Museum of the Cariboo Chilcotin is a regional history museum, and it is home to the BC Cowboy Hall of Fame. If you care about how this region has recorded its own past, it's a place to see that history up close. Find the museum online at cowboy-museum.com, and check there before you plan a visit.

00:09:50.661 --> 00:09:54.981
<v Riley>A museum with objects on shelves is a record a server outage can't wipe.

00:09:55.588 --> 00:10:00.572
<v Casey>That brings us to a painting that sold for a record, and to the question of who keeps its history.

00:10:01.972 --> 00:10:21.756
<v Riley>When a valuable painting changes hands under pressure, and the sale is later disputed, does a settlement close the question? Or should the record of how it left its owner stay open, and who should keep it? Put another way: does the work of holding a record belong to whoever is still standing in the room, or does it need someone from outside?

00:10:22.111 --> 00:10:24.903
<v Casey>Stay open. The price got settled. The history didn't.

00:10:25.577 --> 00:10:32.425
<v Riley>Right. Who owns the painting now is one question. How it left the family is another. The sale answered the first one.

00:10:33.039 --> 00:10:54.335
<v Casey>The painting is Van Gogh's Châtaigniers en fleurs, from 1890. The heirs of Samuel Fischer, a German-Jewish publisher, say the family sold it in Amsterdam in 1936 under Nazi persecution. They asked Sotheby's to pull it from the November 18th sale in New York. The house had valued it at $180 million, the highest estimate for any lot that season.

00:10:54.854 --> 00:11:15.006
<v Riley>On November 17th, Sotheby's announced a "mutually satisfactory resolution" between the current consignors, the Blaquier-Arrieta family, and the legal descendants of Hedwig and Samuel Fischer. The work sold under that agreement. The claim went public days before the sale and was settled before the hammer fell. That's fast.

00:11:15.693 --> 00:11:20.317
<v Casey>Fast, and short. The terms weren't disclosed. The public got a paragraph and no document.

00:11:20.965 --> 00:11:34.381
<v Riley>A paragraph is what a settlement usually looks like. The speed is the point. Pressure on a market works when the claim is visible, and a claim made through press and online campaigns moved a $180 million lot.

00:11:34.997 --> 00:11:55.021
<v Casey>The Guardian put the sale at $190.4 million, a record for a Van Gogh. The Telegraph reported about £145 million. The family's lawyer, Schnabel, said the family hoped the attention would lead to a "fast and fair and just resolution." Two outlets, two currencies, one sale. Whether the resolution was fair, nobody outside the agreement can check.

00:11:55.547 --> 00:12:06.267
<v Riley>Then the buyer paid a record in public, with the claim already on the table. Anyone bidding on provenance-heavy work now knows that question gets asked in public, and at the top of the market.

00:12:06.884 --> 00:12:17.508
<v Casey>Or the seller and the buyer both got what they wanted, and the heirs got a private payment nobody can see. From outside, those two look identical. It's not even clear the heirs were paid at all.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:28.902
<v Riley>They differ in one respect. Before this, there was one account of how the work left the family. Now there are two, the heirs' and the auction house's. Visibility is what lets anyone check either one.

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:40.780
<v Casey>Two accounts is not a record. The heirs' account comes from the family making the claim. The auction house's comes from the party selling the work. Neither one comes from someone who gains nothing from the sale.

00:12:41.374 --> 00:12:49.342
<v Riley>Two interested parties can still check each other. The claim and the statement either match or they don't, and a reader can see the gap.

00:12:49.917 --> 00:13:01.125
<v Casey>Only if the statement says something checkable. "Mutually satisfactory" names no price, no date and no payment. It names two parties and a verdict. That closes the dispute and leaves the route unstated.

00:13:01.747 --> 00:13:08.643
<v Riley>Then the route has to be written somewhere else. The question is who does that, and whether they can afford to do it when nobody is watching.

00:13:09.543 --> 00:13:50.413
<v Riley>The Internet Archive is a useful case, because keeping the record is its whole job. It runs the Wayback Machine, and its mission is what it calls "Universal Access to All Knowledge." It was founded in 1996. More than 80 of its 124 eligible U.S. staffers voted to join the Communications Workers of America, and they are waiting on founder Brewster Kahle to recognize the union voluntarily. The backup is a vote run by the U.S. National Labor Relations Board. Even a mission-driven nonprofit is now a workplace with collective bargaining, and its staff are part of the cultural infrastructure. A nonprofit keeper has no consignor to satisfy.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:14:05.228
<v Casey>No consignor, but a founder. Kahle hadn't responded to WIRED's request for comment when that story ran. The people keeping the record don't have settled terms at their own institution yet, and a mission can make it harder to ask for basic workplace terms.

00:14:05.909 --> 00:14:11.349
<v Riley>Staff who share the mission and can organize make the keeper stronger. They are part of the record.

00:14:11.979 --> 00:14:23.939
<v Casey>Alignment with a mission doesn't tell you the record survives the next budget. The Internet Archive has 124 eligible U.S. staffers. A small collection might have one person who knows where everything is.

00:14:24.459 --> 00:14:39.819
<v Riley>Which is the case for writing it down. The Eleanor Antin dispute from the roundup shows the problem. The party holding the works made the list. Somebody outside the dealer's office had to check it, and the artist's side read it closely enough to find the gap.

00:14:40.433 --> 00:14:50.377
<v Casey>The check caught the gap, but the people checking still work for the artist. Nobody in that chain is neutral. The check worked because someone with a reason to look was reading.

00:14:50.701 --> 00:14:55.109
<v Riley>A reason to look counts for something. Records get tested that way all the time.

00:14:55.626 --> 00:15:06.930
<v Casey>Only while the reader has a job. Take the Mada Masr case from the roundup. When a licence rule decides who may publish, it also decides who keeps the archive, and a confiscation order takes the equipment with it.

00:15:07.555 --> 00:15:23.987
<v Riley>Which is why copies sit outside the building. The archive that matters is the one that survives a raid, a confiscation or a closed budget line. Off-site copies are the cheap answer, and they work for a newsroom in Cairo the same way they work for a museum in Williams Lake.

00:15:24.657 --> 00:15:32.849
<v Casey>The Egyptian court had a legal tool. The Cariboo has a board and a budget. Those are different levers, and the second one is easier to pull quietly.

00:15:33.376 --> 00:15:43.680
<v Riley>Then name the lever. Who can see the file, who can change it, and what happens when the person who holds the key leaves. Write those three answers into the record itself.

00:15:44.298 --> 00:15:53.730
<v Casey>Nieman Lab has the same problem with public media. The Corporation for Public Broadcasting dissolved, and whoever rebuilds it inherits the files and not the staff who knew them.

00:15:54.308 --> 00:16:01.268
<v Riley>That's a reason to keep the staff in the record. A recovered system still needs people who remember how the files were arranged.

00:16:01.839 --> 00:16:08.359
<v Casey>Agreed on that. I don't accept that a rebuilt system fixes itself. It needs a line item, the same as the one it lost.

00:16:08.948 --> 00:16:40.490
<v Riley>So build the line item in. Now bring it home. The Museum of the Cariboo Chilcotin in Williams Lake is a regional history museum, and the BC Cowboy Hall of Fame sits inside it. A museum like that holds objects that came from many donors over many years. The entry for each one can be short: who gave it, when, and under what conditions. Dated, signed and reviewed. The museum already asks donors to fill out a donation form and give provenance details, so the habit exists. Any collection can adopt it and show it.

00:16:41.092 --> 00:16:57.748
<v Casey>It can. But in a regional collection, some of those stories are already gone. The donor may be long gone, the paperwork may sit in a drawer, and the one person who remembered the handover may have retired. A form won't recover a story nobody wrote down. The entry should say plainly what is unknown.

00:16:58.405 --> 00:17:03.877
<v Riley>An entry that says "unknown" is still a record. A blank filled with a guess is the bigger risk.

00:17:04.478 --> 00:17:14.942
<v Casey>Who checks the "unknown"? If the museum writes its own entries and also holds the objects, it's the seller and the keeper in one room. Same conflict as the auction house, in a smaller room.

00:17:15.512 --> 00:17:25.240
<v Riley>Then the list needs an outside reader. Public, dated, and with the gaps stated. Put it under a named keeper with a review date, and it survives the volunteer who wrote it.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:32.988
<v Casey>Named keeper, review date. Fine. Who keeps the keeper's job when the budget line goes? That's the part I'd want written down.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:44.942
<v Riley>The same logic reaches the shop shelf. Mama C's Gifts &amp; Giggles and The Crafty Nook carry local makers' jewellery and honey, and each piece has a maker whose name should travel with it.

00:17:45.616 --> 00:17:53.632
<v Casey>Only if somebody writes the name on the tag. Whatever a shop's first owner knew about each maker leaves with the sale, unless someone wrote it down.

00:17:54.171 --> 00:18:01.371
<v Riley>Then the write-down is cheap. A name, a date and a sentence on the tag. The shelf can keep the record the way a museum can.

00:18:01.887 --> 00:18:10.911
<v Casey>Cheap for the shelf. For a museum with a hundred years of donors, the sentence on the tag is the expensive part, because someone has to go find the sentence first.

00:18:11.599 --> 00:18:16.127
<v Riley>My pick is the collection that holds the object, with an outside reader who can check it.

00:18:16.792 --> 00:18:26.504
<v Casey>I'd put it with a neutral archive. I don't have one picked out for the Cariboo, and the seller already has a record. It's the one that produced the two-sentence announcement.

00:18:27.033 --> 00:18:40.785
<v Riley>Then the test is whether an entry can say "unknown" and still be trusted. If you have a correction, a story tip, or a view on who should keep these records, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Have a great rest of your day.

00:18:41.326 --> 00:18:49.998
<v Casey>That's the test I want to see before anyone calls a record settled. Go look at the shop counters on the way home. Buy something made nearby, and ask who made it.

00:18:50.598 --> 00:19:09.950
